        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Most demos work. That's the trap. A demo is a controlled environment with a known input, a friendly audience, and a fifteen-minute attention budget. It tells you almost nothing about whether the thing will survive contact with a real user."
          }
        ],
        "role": "body",
        "fontSize": 22,
        "fontWeight": 400,
        "align": "left",
        "pinnedStyle": {
          "color": "#f1ece0"
        },
        "lineHeight": 1.55
      },
      {
        "id": "pf-post1-p1",
        "type": "text",
        "box": {
          "x": 240,
          "y": 248,
          "w": 720,
          "h": 120,
          "z": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Week three is when the honest signal arrives. By then the launch energy has burned off, the bug surface has not, and the user has discovered all the affordances you forgot to build. If the thing is still in their workflow at week three, you built something. If it isn't, the demo lied."
          }
        ],
        "role": "body",
        "fontSize": 17,
        "fontWeight": 400,
        "align": "left",
        "pinnedStyle": {
